    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nSince 3.3.0b1, calling `.pop(key, default)` on a dict annotated with `int` values and **discarding the result** generates C that no compiler accepts:\n\n```c\nif (__Pyx_PyInt_FromNumber(&((PyObject *)__pyx_t_4), NULL, 1) < (0)) __PYX_ERR(0, 3, __pyx_L1_error)\n```\n\nThe address-of operator is applied to a cast expression, which is not an lvalue.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour\n\n```python\n# repro.py\ndef f(counts: dict[str, int], keys: list[str]) -> None:\n    for k in keys:\n        counts.pop(k, None)\n```\n\n```console\n$ pip install cython==3.3.0 setuptools\n$ cythonize -3 -i repro.py\nrepro.c: In function '__pyx_pf_5repro_f':\nrepro.c:2741:32: error: lvalue required as unary '&' operand\nerror: Command '['gcc', ..., '-c', 'repro.c', ...]' returned non-zero exit status 1.\n```\n\nNo extension module is produced; `cythonize` exits 1.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nValid C, as produced by 3.2.9 for the same input.\n\n### Environment\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| OS | Debian 13 (`python:3.12-slim` container) and macOS 15 (arm64) |\n| Python | 3.12 |\n| Compilers | gcc 14.2.0 and clang 17 — both reject the generated C |\n| Cython | **3.2.9 — OK**, **3.3.0b1 — fails**, **3.3.0 — fails** |\n\nControl run in the same container: same file, same command, `cython==3.2.9` → exit 0, extension module produced.\n\n### Notes that may help\n\nThe failure is narrow — all three conditions are required:\n\n1. the dict is annotated with `int` values (`dict[str, str]` is fine),\n2. `pop` is called with a default (`pop(k, None)`),\n3. **the result is discarded** — assigning it (`x = counts.pop(k, None)`) produces valid C.\n\nDropping the annotation also produces valid C, which suggests the int-typed optimisation path is involved. The generated helper is `__Pyx_PyInt_FromNumber`, which appears to come from the \"Skip Py -> C -> Py Coercion\" work (#7735) — possibly an edge case where the destination is a temporary rather than a variable.\n\nWe hit this in a production build: the compile failure was non-fatal in our Dockerfile, so the image shipped without the module. Not your problem, but it is why the report is a bit detailed — the failure is easy to miss in CI.\n",
